WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.390
The Matrix movies are so packed with meaning
that every line seems to carry some deeper

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:05.960
significance.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:10.220
In that spirit, we're looking at some of the
most memorable lines from the Matrix films

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:12.860
that have more way meaning than anyone realized.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:19.540
Neo's first encounter with his primary adversary,
Agent Smith, arrives early in the first Matrix,

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:22.600
before Neo's been clued into everything that's
really going on.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:28.680
It's part of an interrogation, in which Smith
freaks Neo out with a variety of threats,

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.460
including this seemingly simple statement:

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:36.580
"You're going to help us, Mr. Anderson, whether
you want to or not."

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.380
What the franchise hasn't yet established
is that, even though it sounds like Smith

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:45.600
is just playing bad cop, he's actually quite
correct, at least in theory.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:51.760
We learn later in The Matrix Reloaded that
Neo's status as the One is actually a piece

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:52.620
of programming.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:57.400
It was implanted in him by the Architect as
part of a plan that will allow the Matrix

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:01.560
to reboot and preserve the appearance of choice
among the human race.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:07.420
At this point in the story, Neo is unknowingly
a tool of the Machines and the Matrix, and

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:10.020
he won't figure that out until part two.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:16.420
After Neo meets Morpheus and agrees to take
the red pill, he awakens in his pod among

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:19.580
the human farm and is taken aboard Morpheus'
ship.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:25.070
As he learns the truth while on board the
Nebuchadnezzar, he asks why his eyes hurt.

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:28.280
The answer Morpheus provides is simple and
straightforward:

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:30.380
"You've never used them before."

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:35.400
That explanation is technically true, because
Neo's biological form has been locked up inside

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.500
that gel-filled pod for decades.

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:43.340
But his question also offers some interesting
foreshadowing for the future of his eyes.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:49.200
The topic comes up again in The Matrix Reloaded
when the Merovingian, aware that Neo can now

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.800
see the Matrix's code, asks:

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.580
"You can see it, Neo, yes?"

00:01:55.080 --> 00:02:01.100
Then, in The Matrix Revolutions, Agent Smith,
while in Bane's body, blinds Neo with an electrical

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:05.380
cable, causing intense pain but also giving
him a new level of sight.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:11.390
His eyes are useless, but he can still see
the energies around him as pure light, which

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:14.580
he uses to make his final journey to the Machine
City.

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:15.460
"Oh no.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:17.060
Your eyes."

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:19.020
"I'll be okay."

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:24.640
After Neo is woken up and pulled from his
pod, he begins his training under Morpheus

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:25.580
and his crew.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:30.700
This includes sessions with Tank, who plugs
Neo into his system and uploads a variety

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:35.060
of training programs into his brain, including
skills in various martial arts.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:40.519
Neo is immediately thrilled by this process,
and he endures hours of uploading at Tank's

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:41.060
hands.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:44.920
When Morpheus comes to check on him, Tank
says of Neo:

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:46.300
"Ten hours straight.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:47.520
He's a machine."

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:52.319
Tank is, of course, simply referring to Neo's
endurance as he undergoes the strain of the

00:02:52.319 --> 00:02:54.560
uploading process hour after hour.

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:59.080
But he's also actually weirdly close to the
truth of Neo's actual identity.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:04.879
Though we don't learn this until The Matrix
Reloaded, Neo is actually a tool of the Machines,

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:06.460
while not being a Machine himself.

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:11.300
He was designed to be an asset to them, to
help in the rebooting of the Matrix and the

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:14.280
further use of humanity as a docile power
source.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:20.000
In a sense, the Machines built Neo and coded
him for their own purposes, so in that sense,

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:21.800
Tank is fully correct.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:27.480
Shortly after Neo learns that the Matrix is
nothing but a simulation, he spends some time

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:32.440
contemplating his old life, which he thought
was real but was actually all in his head.

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:37.340
As he struggles with coming to terms with
that truth, he asks Trinity what it means

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:39.700
that all of his memories never happened.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:41.940
And that's when she replies:

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:44.320
"That the Matrix cannot tell you who you are."

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:49.740
Trinity's response is based on her belief
in the cause of freedom for humanity.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:54.540
She wants to condition Neo to the new reality
that he's living in, which will allow him

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:58.080
to exercise his own free will in ways he never
could before.

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:02.820
What's interesting, though, is that while
she turns out to be correct, the Matrix does

00:04:02.829 --> 00:04:07.569
spend a good deal of time in the course of
the sequels trying to tell Neo who he is.

00:04:07.569 --> 00:04:12.420
A large portion of Reloaded is devoted to
characters like the Architect attempting to

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:18.130
explain to Neo that he was programmed to reboot
the Matrix and that he is not unique.

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:23.570
There have been other Ones before, and there
will be other Ones after, if all goes according

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:24.140
to plan.

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:29.740
The Matrix can tell you who you are, but Neo
must overcome that fact to achieve peace.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:36.060
Early in The Matrix, Neo first meets the Oracle,
who tells him matter-of-factly that he isn't

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:36.800
the One.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:41.880
He eventually proves this statement wrong
by saving Morpheus and defeating the Agents

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:42.900
at the end of the film.

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:47.840
When he tries to protest that he can't be
the One because the Oracle said so, Morpheus

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:51.460
explains that she told him exactly what he
needed to hear.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.580
"Neo, sooner or later you’re going to realize,
just as I did, that there’s a difference

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:59.320
between knowing the path and walking the path."

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:04.180
Neo spends much of the trilogy struggling
with this fact, especially since he's living

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:08.060
in a world with prophecies and an old woman
who seems to know his future.

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:12.900
He asks a lot of questions in an effort to
determine exactly what he must do.

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:18.220
Some people, including the Architect, attempt
to lay his path out for him very clearly in

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:19.600
an effort to manipulate him.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:24.900
He tells him that if he goes through the door
that allows him to save Trinity, he will effectively

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:28.160
end the human race, but Neo goes anyway.

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:33.380
He walks the path instead of attempting to
know it, and finds a third way that allows

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:36.260
him to save both Trinity and the future of
Zion.

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:40.380
"If I were you, I would hope that we don't
meet again."

